<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      User BFF schema. Liquibase runs over a JDBC datasource (Liquibase has no
      R2DBC runner); all runtime data access goes through R2DBC. Every table is
      owner-keyed by the Casdoor JWT subject (user_id) — a user only ever touches
      their own rows (owner-enforcement lives in the service layer too).

        001 — user_profile     (own profile: gender/pronouns/tz/displayName/visibility/vanity)
        002 — user_preference  (own per-key preferences incl. home-guide-dismissed)
        003 — user_block       (own self-blocks pushed to Matrix/Mastodon/internal)
        004 — user_profile.vanity_username + reserved-name CHECK (vanity collision guard)
        005 — enum/domain CHECK constraints (DB parity for the API-enforced
              user_profile.visibility + user_block.platform vocabularies)
        006 — self_silence (self-service visibility hide; NOT a ban — zero Casdoor
              roles / access_suspension / revocation keys; @Scheduled auto-clear)
        007 — media_poison_decision (server-owned AI-cloak decision + outcome for
              the BFF media-proxy: audit + badge + repost source of truth)
        008 — user_profile.screen_reader_name (ASCII-only a11y aria-label name;
              defaults from an ASCII display_name, prompted when display_name is
              non-ASCII)
        009 — connected_client_audit (owner-scoped record of the user's OAuth apps /
              device sessions on the homeservers + revoke audit)
        010 — domain scoping: a `domain` column (DEFAULT 'default', NOT NULL) on every
              owner-keyed table = the brand/tenant the row belongs to, derived
              server-side from the validated token issuer (DOMAIN_<x>). A principal
              only sees/mutates rows in its own domain; SUPERADMIN is unscoped.
              Back-compat: existing rows + single-issuer clusters are all 'default'.
        011 — user_preferences_store: the DURABLE per-user UI preferences store the
              /bff/user/preferences endpoint reads/upserts. Domain-FIRST: the brand is
              part of the composite PK (user_id, pref_key, domain) so a multi-brand
              owner keeps a separate, owner-isolated preference per brand (carries the
              per-user media.poison.default the compose AI-poison toggle persists).
        012 — notifications PR1: the in-app notification FLOOR
              (user_notifications) + per-category PREFERENCES
              (notification_preference). Both owner-keyed (user_subject) + domain-
              scoped; prefs MIRROR 011 (domain-first composite PK). account-restriction
              is FORCED-ON (un-silenceable) — API-coerced + a DB CHECK backstop. SHIPS
              DARK: nothing writes user_notifications yet (the snapdragon-actions
              reconciler in a later PR is the only writer); this is the read/prefs side.
        013 — D6 hs_service_token: the SERVER-SIDE CUSTODY STORE for homeserver-NATIVE
              Matrix/Mastodon access tokens this BFF mints + holds per user. One row
              per (owner_subject, platform) UNIQUE; access/refresh tokens AES-256-GCM
              at rest (HsTokenCipher, key from app.hs-token.key via ESO/Vault), NEVER
              plaintext / NEVER returned to a client. SHIPS DARK: read/written only
              when app.hs-token.enabled=true (default false); the legacy admin-bff
              /bff/provision/* browser-token path stays the live fallback.
        014 — user_profile.avatar_url (the profile picture as a Matrix mxc:// URL).
              Server-derived by the dedicated avatar endpoint from the homeserver
              media-upload result, NEVER from the profile PUT body, and never cleared
              by a profile save (the main upsert omits the column). SHIPS DARK: the
              live Matrix media upload is stub-gated (avatar.matrix.enabled, default
              false) — a stub mxc:// is returned so storage works without a live
              homeserver; the column is the durable source of truth either way.
        015 — DB-generated UUID primary keys (adopt mod-bff #36 / admin-bff #133):
              add a (gen_random_uuid())::text column DEFAULT to the five varchar(36/64)
              String-PK tables (user_block, connected_client_audit, media_poison_decision,
              user_notifications, hs_service_token) so Postgres mints the id; the app stops
              calling UUID.randomUUID() and reads the id back via INSERT ... RETURNING id.
              pgcrypto is gated to Postgres; the DEFAULT changeSets are harmless under H2
              (never evaluated by the non-migrated H2 slices).
        016 — vn_character_part: a USER-DRAWN character PART (an SVG) for the VN/character-
              studio drawable-parts feature. The stored `svg` is the SERVER-SANITIZED
              subset (SvgSanitizerService runs BEFORE storage), saved status='PENDING',
              and the save files a held @system moderation finding (surface=character-part)
              so a HUMAN reviews the sanitized SVG itself (no server-side rasterizer). DB-
              generated UUID PK ((gen_random_uuid())::text, 015 idiom); owner_subject +
              domain scoped like every other owner-keyed table; status CHECK parity.
        017 — vn_sticker_pack: a VN-CHARACTER EXPRESSION STICKER (Phase B1, increment 1 —
              the moderated sticker-pack STORAGE backend). One row per per-user expression
              sticker: a raster (PNG) snapshot rendered from the character rig at a
              canonical EXPRESSION (StickerExpression vocabulary). The save leg is
              MODERATE → STORE-PENDING → FILE-MODERATION-FINDING: the PNG bytes are edge-AI
              vision-moderated FIRST (reuse of the avatar EdgeAiAnalysisClient.moderate,
              fail-CLOSED when enabled-but-unreachable; allow-all when dark), a blocked
              sticker never reaches the homeserver and is never persisted (422), an allowed
              one is uploaded to the homeserver media repo (mxc://, stub-gated like the
              avatar) and persisted status='PENDING', and the save files a held @system
              moderation finding (surface=character-sticker). DB-generated UUID PK
              ((gen_random_uuid())::text, 015 idiom); owner_subject + domain scoped; status
              + expression CHECK parity (the expression CHECK mirrors StickerExpression.ALL).
        018 — vn_character_part.finding_id + vn_sticker_pack.finding_id: the receiving end of
              the part/sticker ACTIVATION loop. A NULLABLE bigint finding_id (the federation
              @system finding id this row filed, federation-api #70 subjectRef) is stamped
              best-effort AFTER the row insert so a later moderator resolution of that finding
              can flip the owning row PENDING→ACTIVE/REJECTED. NOT a hard FK (the finding lives
              in another service); an index supports the reconciler's row-for-finding lookup.
              The SERVICE-gated /bff/user/internal/character flip endpoint is the consumer.
        019 — mesh_device: a Meshtastic LoRa radio node CLAIMED by a platform user so the
              mesh↔Matrix bridge can attribute that radio's messages to them. node_id is
              stored CANONICAL ("!"+8 lowercase hex, service-normalized, regex CHECK on
              Postgres) and UNIQUE ALONE — one owner per radio platform-wide (a duplicate
              claim is a 409). Carries the owner-managed status visibility
              (private|friends|channels), the bridge-written LoRa status columns
              (last_seen_at/battery_pct 0..101/snr/hops_away/via_device — SERVICE-gated
              batch write-back, ACTIVE rows only, fail-soft), and the moderation state
              columns (state active|revoked + revoked_by/revoked_at/revoke_reason; the
              mod-bff revocation WRITER is a later wave — a revoked row is excluded from
              the bridge mapping, its visibility frozen, and its node_id stays occupied
              so revocation can't be dodged by re-claiming). DB-generated UUID PK
              ((gen_random_uuid())::text, 015 idiom); owner_subject + domain scoped;
              visibility/state/battery CHECK parity.
    -->

